#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172909} :activity/id #uuid "029bded9-b237-543e-8c70-78a922e3ce4f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048655 :topic/id #uuid "b2c456e2-ef0a-5a75-95da-68bb5f61da4a" :topic/math-academy-id 3356} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173163 :tutorial/content "A  plane curve \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r:[a,b]\\to \\Bbb R^2\n$$\n\nis said to be **simple** if it doesn’t intersect itself anywhere between its endpoints. Notice that  this means that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t_1)\\neq \\mathbf r(t_2), \\qquad \na < t_1< t_2 < b.\n$$\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is simple while curve II is not simple.\n\n![](images/7b/7be1bc23d37c187a642d51917d6acb846147059bc6e9978d6ccd4ed35eed8794.png)\n\nA  plane curve is said to be **closed** if its terminal point coincides with its initial point, that is, if \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(b)= \\mathbf r(a).\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b21a6e638cb8b0bae8967ba142fbfacb14349f34ff80d9359643e03ea1c1f83.png)\n\nFor example, curve III shown below is closed, while curve IV is not:\n\n![](images/85/8507fb039da42f37307ca3994cfb5ad66b288fb28d0cfd1a1a179acd2f83ae5e.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Curve III is an example of both a closed and simple curve. Notice that an example of a closed but *not* simple curve looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86dcd1dc864a2f0e2ceade072ba230b8fb4fe8923189a7eed2964f6f66a67651.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "72ccdc89-4807-5abb-9f69-7e017f195c3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5100 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bafdfe3-0f81-5668-ad79-241cf2fc2b91" :step/math-academy-id 32008 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172916}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171853 :question/id #uuid "0bf9999b-16dd-567b-9e52-e80a1b0c6a38" :question/math-academy-id "e-8675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "325cfa6e-a3ff-51f3-9667-95e4ad86ac4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172660} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395507}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Simple Curves"} :step/id #uuid "8a0f9d7f-ab53-56ab-bdd3-8439d8f9ce7a" :step/math-academy-id 32200 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172917}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172917 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171854 :question/id #uuid "ee0133c9-056e-5595-b407-8f8f00c49823" :question/math-academy-id "e-8676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ca16ec8-183d-5e2e-9715-24107f8aa8c5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395493}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Closed Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b500b6d6-4ad6-5209-ba0d-8f0be8ba230d" :step/math-academy-id 32201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172929}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172918 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171855 :question/id #uuid "1b8cbd6a-e306-5092-a402-6a0da61c5ec8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395500}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "01467366-cedf-5b66-ab24-41950e76376f" :step/math-academy-id 32202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172929 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173171 :tutorial/content "A very important (although very hard to prove) result in mathematics states the following:\n\nAny simple and closed curve divides the plane into exactly two regions, one inside the curve and one outside.\n\nBased on this fact, we can define the **orientation** of a simple closed curve as follows:\n\nA simple closed curve is **positively oriented** if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is **negatively oriented**.\n\nFor example, curve I shown below is positively oriented, while curve II is negatively oriented:\n\n![](images/95/9527dd8bdfd979a1d521ccf945e84ef9b557d67e3d89ce8f5e4f7bb8d287ee8c.png)\n\nAn alternative convenient way of looking at simple oriented curves is that a curve is positively oriented if it is traversed *counterclockwise* and it is negatively oriented if it is traversed *clockwise*." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c2c2591-a914-53a3-9f60-6e7335d049f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5165 :tutorial/title "Oriented Curves"} :step/id #uuid "2b76b9e7-6d5f-584c-837f-bd003bd3c835" :step/math-academy-id 32465 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172918}}] :activity/title "Simple, Closed, and Oriented Curves" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172178}]}
